@participant7 Here's the reason why `fcmv1=false` for killed notifications on Android are not working, and possibly other cases where people are getting strange null notifications being returned.

When using `fcmv1=false`, the extras being passed here are still stored as byte array under `notificationResponse`.

```
  override fun onNotificationResponseIntentReceived(extras: Bundle?) {
    lastNotificationResponseBundle = NotificationSerializer.toResponseBundleFromExtras(extras)
    sendEvent(NEW_RESPONSE_EVENT_NAME, lastNotificationResponseBundle)
  }
```

Which means the calls to `getString()` in `NotificationSerializer.java::toResponseBundleFromExtras` all return null, resulting in an empty notification like this:


```
{
  "actionIdentifier": "expo.modules.notifications.actions.DEFAULT",
  "notification": {
    "date": 0,
    "request": {
      "identifier": null,
      "content": {
        "body": null,
        "dataString": null,
        "title": null
      },
      "trigger": {
        "type": "push",
        "channelId": null
      }
    }
  }
}
```

When using `fcmv1=true`, `NotificationResponse` is correctly unmarshaled and calls to `getString()` work fine.

I would add that the payload of the notification being received when using `fcmv1=false` and the app is killed *is valid*.  You can see that by adding logging to `NotificationManager` `onNotificationResponseReceived()`. 

Would it be possible to get a fix for this? It would really simplify production rollout, since we'd have both legacy API support both for older builds and those who take the update to support `fcmv1`.

