@participant13 @participant7 @participant14 With these two patches, I have everything working on both Android for legacy AND fcmv1, that includes icons and notifications in all states (background/foreground/killed).

This patch is to get Android killed notifications working when `fcmv1=false` with `expo-notifications@0.28.6` (it will work in later versions too). It does not require the use of the original client side hack as per https://github.com/expo/expo/issues/14078

```
--- a/node_modules/expo-notifications/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/notifications/service/delegates/ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener.java
+++ b/node_modules/expo-notifications/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/notifications/service/delegates/ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener.java
@@ -41,6 +41,15 @@ public class ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener implements ReactActivityLifecycle
         if (intent != null) {
             Bundle extras = intent.getExtras();
             if (extras != null) {
+                // this is a workaround for a bug which causes legacy notifications (fcmv1=false)
+                // to fail when the app is in a killed state.
+                // for some reason, extras here is a bundle with notificationResponse as a byte key array
+                // rather than an unmarhsaled object containing the notification payload.
+                // pushing this notification into the stack results in the return of a null notification on the client.
+                // alas, if this key exists, skip adding it to our list of notification responses
+                // as the notification seems to come in later just fine.
+                if (extras.containsKey("notificationResponse")) {
+                    Log.d("ReactNativeJS", "[native] ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener contains an unmarshaled notification response. Skipping.");
+                    return;
+                }
                 mNotificationManager.onNotificationResponseFromExtras(extras);
             }
         }
```

You will need `patch-package` installed: put that in `patches` as `expo-notifications+0.28.6.patch` and then run `yarn && npx expo run:android` to rebuild the Android bundle.

I don't know what causes the extras here to be unmarshaled, but it looks like there's been an issue with this before:

https://github.com/expo/expo/blob/29ee21a3b162e341f739a898dbf6c34b2efdc6aa/packages/expo-notifications/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/notifications/service/NotificationsService.kt#L519

In theory, this patch is safe, as this key should never exist in this response. You'll know when the condition is met as this line will be logged to the console:

`[native] ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener contains an unmarshaled notification response. Skipping.`

@participant13 maybe try giving this a go and seeing if it fixes your issue? For me, all notifications are working on both APIs now.

To get icons working on fcmv1 and legacy, you will need to add these 4 entries to your `AndroidManifest.xml`:

```
<meta-data android:name="expo.modules.notifications.default_notification_color" android:resource="@color/notification_icon_color"/>
<meta-data android:name="expo.modules.notifications.default_notification_icon" android:resource="@drawable/notification_icon"/>
<meta-data android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_color" android:resource="@color/notification_icon_color"/>
<meta-data android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_icon" android:resource="@drawable/notification_icon"/>
```

Technically this could be automated by patching this file https://github.com/expo/expo/blob/e377f0cd22db5cd7feb8e80348cd7064db5429b1/packages/expo-notifications/plugin/build/withNotificationsAndroid.js . That plugin assumes only one API will be used for a given build, which is wrong, as we're trying to handle a graceful switchover between APIs.

