For the Android patch, after running `yarn`, inspect `node_modules/expo-notifications/android/src/main/java/expo/modules/notifications/service/delegates/ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener.java` and check that `onCreate` contains the `if (extras.containsKey("notificationResponse")` statement. Here is what it looks like:

```java
  /**
     * This will be triggered if the app is not running,
     * and is started from clicking on a notification.
     * <p>
     * Notification data will be in activity.intent.extras
     *
     * @param activity
     * @param savedInstanceState
     */
    @Override
    public void onCreate(Activity activity, Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        Intent intent = activity.getIntent();
        if (intent != null) {
            Bundle extras = intent.getExtras();
            if (extras != null) {
                // this is a workaround for a bug which causes legacy notifications (fcmv1=false)
                // to fail when the app is in a killed state.
                // for some reason, extras here is a bundle with notificationResponse as a byte key array
                // rather than an unmarhsaled object containing the notification payload.
                // pushing this notification into the stack results in the return of a null notification on the client.
                // alas, if this key exists, skip adding it to our list of notification responses
                // as the notification seems to come in later just fine.
                if (extras.containsKey("notificationResponse")) {
                    Log.d("ReactNativeJS", "[native] ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener contains unmarshaled notification response. Skipping.");
                    return;
                }
                mNotificationManager.onNotificationResponseFromExtras(extras);
            }
        }
    }
```

You can test it by firing off a legacy notification with the app killed. When you tap the notification, you should see this log:

`[native] ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener contains an unmarshaled notification response. Skipping.`

And most importantly, you should get the full notification response - without the patch, you will get a notification with null properties.

