@participant7 this is a fix for this bug:

1.  Open app > background it
2. Send a fcmv1 notification using the expo tool: https://expo.dev/notifications (or as per @participant13 , a php implementation of the expo notification server)
3. Tap the notification

result: notification not received in the client.

This does not repro for me using nodejs `expo-server-sdk@3.9.0`. When using that API, this notification comes in unmarshaled via `ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener.onNewIntent()` (which is skipped) but is then picked up by the listeners in the `NotificationManager`. 

However, when using the expo tool or the php implementation, it comes through as a valid notification, but it is not picked up by the listeners. Instead, it is passed through to `ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener.onNewIntent()` and then to `onNotificationResponseFromExtras()`. However, there's an issue with that method, as described below, which stops these notifications being sent.

Here is that patch & explanation separated from the other ones:

```java

// old

 public void onNotificationResponseFromExtras(Bundle extras) {
   // bug exists here - if we have listeners already setup and 
   // we're processing a notification from a background state
   // this notification will be added to mPendingNotificationResponsesFromExtras 
   // and never gets processed
    if (mPendingNotificationResponsesFromExtras.isEmpty()) {
      mPendingNotificationResponsesFromExtras.add(extras);
    } else {
      for (WeakReference<NotificationListener> listenerReference : mListenerReferenceMap.values()) {
        NotificationListener listener = listenerReference.get();
        if (listener != null) {
          listener.onNotificationResponseIntentReceived(extras);
        }
      }
    }
  }

// patched

 public void onNotificationResponseFromExtras(Bundle extras) {
    // We're going to be passed in extras from either
    // a killed state (ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener::onCreate)
    // OR a background state (ExpoNotificationLifecycleListener::onNewIntent)

    // If we've just come from a background state, we'll have listeners set up
    // pass on the notification to them
    if (!mListenerReferenceMap.isEmpty()) {
      for (WeakReference<NotificationListener> listenerReference : mListenerReferenceMap.values()) {
        NotificationListener listener = listenerReference.get();
        if (listener != null) {
          listener.onNotificationResponseIntentReceived(extras);
        }
      }
    } else {
      // Otherwise, the app has been launched from a killed state, and our listeners
      // haven't yet been setup. We'll add this to a list of pending notifications
      // for them to process once they've been initialized.
      if (mPendingNotificationResponsesFromExtras.isEmpty()) {
        mPendingNotificationResponsesFromExtras.add(extras);
      }
    }
  }
```

@participant7 I have no clue why these two server implementations are resulting in different results like this. But given that one results in an unmarshaled notification (which triggers listeners) whilst the other produces a valid notification (but doesn't trigger listeners!) may shed some light on the underlying issue here.

